import { control as C } from './authored-lesson-seeds.mjs';
export const profiles=new Map();
function P(key,definition,why,method,memory,formula,restriction,controls,second,target,mistake,correction,prompt){profiles.set(key,{definition,why,method,memory,formula,restriction,controls,second,target,mistake,correction,prompt});}
P('fermi','A Fermi estimate breaks a large question into a few quantities that can be estimated and multiplied. It gives a plausible scale, not an exact count.','A useful estimate exposes its assumptions. The calculation can be exact even when the inputs are uncertain. Small percentage uncertainties in separate factors combine multiplicatively, so two 10% increases give a 21% product increase rather than 20%.','Choose a count of groups and an average number per group. Explain the basis for each estimate, include units, and check a lower and upper scenario. Round only after calculating, then ask which uncertain input matters most.','Estimate the pieces; multiply; state the uncertainty.','Total = groups × average per group','The uncertainty band assumes each positive input is within 10%; it is not a measured confidence interval.',[C('a','Number of groups',30,1,100),C('b','Items per group',20,1,100)],{a:40,b:15},'Estimate','An estimate is an exact measured answer.','A computed product inherits uncertainty from its inputs. State the assumptions and a reasonable range.','Estimate the total items from {a} groups with {b} items per group.');
P('indian-place','Indian large-number names group places as ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, lakhs and crores. One lakh is 100000 and one crore is 10000000.','The numerical value is independent of the comma convention. Indian grouping keeps the final three digits together and groups the remaining digits in pairs. Place-value notation uses zero to hold empty places so 1005 differs from 105.','Read the place groups from the right. Convert a named quantity to its underlying number before arithmetic. Divide by 100000 for lakhs or 10000000 for crores. Reconstruct the number to check a conversion.','Names group digits; powers of ten give their value.','1 lakh=10⁵; 1 crore=10⁷','Use whole-number values; grouping conventions do not change magnitude.',[C('n','Whole-number value',2500000,1,100000000)],{n:73000000},'Lakhs','Changing comma placement changes the number.','Commas are reading aids. A conversion changes the unit of description, not the counted quantity.','Express {n} as a number of lakhs.');
P('base-system','A positional system gives a digit its value from both its symbol and its position. In base b the places are 1, b, b², b³ and so on.','Only digits 0 through b−1 are allowed. A zero digit holds an empty place: 101 in base 2 means one four, zero twos and one unit. Without its position, a digit alone cannot tell the full contribution.','To decode, multiply each digit by its place weight and add. To encode a nonnegative integer, repeatedly divide by the base and read the remainders backwards. Check by converting the representation back to the original number.','Position supplies a power; the digit counts that power.','N=Σdᵢbⁱ','This tool uses ordinary integer bases 2–36. Letters A–Z represent digits 10–35; historical exceptions require their own model.',[C('n','Decimal whole number',143,0,10000),C('base','Representation base',5,2,36)],{n:45,base:2},'Representation','Every numeral system uses exactly the same place weights.','Egyptian additive notation and calendar-style Mayan weights illustrate why the convention must be stated.','Write decimal {n} in base {base}.');
P('roman','Roman notation uses landmark symbols I, V, X, L, C, D and M. A numeral combines additive groups and conventional subtractive groups such as IV or IX.','The position of a symbol can affect whether it adds or forms a subtractive pair, but it is not a general power-of-ten place-value digit. Modern conventional Roman notation has no zero digit and does not represent every signed or decimal number.','Work from the largest permitted group downward. After choosing a group, subtract its value and continue. Decode the completed numeral to check the total. Use only the conventional subtractive pairs; arbitrary smaller-before-larger combinations are not valid.','Read groups, not decimal places.','IV=4, IX=9, XL=40, XC=90, CD=400, CM=900','Modern conventional form; integers 1–3999 only. Historical usage was not always uniform.',[C('n','Positive integer',1984,1,3999)],{n:944},'Roman numeral','Any small symbol can be subtracted from any larger symbol.','The conventional form permits particular pairs. For 49 use XLIX, not IL.','Write {n} using the stated Roman convention.');
P('egyptian','Additive Egyptian-style notation counts repeated landmark symbols. A symbol can stand for one, ten, a hundred or another power of ten; their contributions add.','This example helps distinguish an additive system from a positional one. Ten units can be exchanged for one ten-symbol, but moving a one-symbol to another place does not by itself turn it into a hundred. The tool displays symbol counts rather than reproducing ancient glyphs.','Decompose the value into landmark multiples. Record the count of each landmark and add the contributions to verify the total. Compare how many symbols an additive representation needs with the number of positional digits.','Add the landmark values; do not read position as a digit weight.','N=Σ count × landmark','Illustrates powers-of-ten additive representation through 99999; historical glyph forms are not simulated.',[C('n','Value to represent',2341,1,99999)],{n:999},'Symbol count','A ten-symbol becomes a hundred-symbol when moved left.','In an additive system its designated value stays the same. A positional system works differently.','How many landmark symbols are needed to represent {n} in this additive model?');
P('mayan','A numeral system must state its place weights. The calendar-style Mayan representation illustrated here uses units, twenties, 360s and 7200s.','The jump from 20 to 360 is 18×20 rather than 20×20. It is therefore not the same as an ordinary base-20 expansion at every position. Recognising this exception prevents a plausible-looking but wrong conversion.','Start with the largest displayed weight. Count how many fit, subtract their contribution, and continue with the next weight. Add the contributions afterwards. Compare with ordinary base 20 only after stating which system the question uses.','Check the third place: 360 or 400?','Calendar-style weights: 1,20,360,7200','This is a limited calendar-style place-weight model, not a claim that all Mayan writing used these same weights.',[C('n','Value to decompose',743,0,10000)],{n:421},'Units digit','The third place is always 20².','Calendar-style weights have an 18 multiplier at that step; ordinary base-20 notation does not.','Find the units count in the displayed calendar-style representation of {n}.');
const quadControls=[C('a','First adjacent side',6,1,12),C('b','Second adjacent side',4,1,12),C('angle','Included angle in degrees',90,20,160)];
P('quadrilateral','Quadrilaterals are closed figures with four straight sides. Particular side, angle and diagonal conditions identify special classes.','A rectangle is a parallelogram with right angles; a rhombus is a parallelogram with equal sides. A square satisfies both. Properties must not be confused with a picture that only looks like a special case: a diagram can suggest a claim, while congruence and angle reasoning prove it.','State the given properties, identify which class they force, and justify each conclusion. In the tool a parallelogram is built from two sides and an included angle. Try equal sides, a right angle, or both; then compare the diagonals and area.','Equal sides gives rhombus; right angle gives rectangle; both gives square.','Area=ab sinθ; perimeter=2(a+b)','The tool constructs parallelograms only, not arbitrary quadrilaterals. Floating diagonal lengths are approximate; special-case classification uses the chosen side/angle values.',quadControls,{a:5,b:5,angle:90},'Perimeter','A rhombus must have right angles.','Equal sides alone do not fix its angles. A non-square rhombus is a counterexample.','Find the perimeter of the parallelogram with adjacent sides {a} and {b}.');
P('diagonal-rectangle','Equal diagonal segments that bisect each other determine a rectangle when their endpoints are joined in cyclic order.','Bisection makes the quadrilateral a parallelogram. In a parallelogram, equal diagonals force adjacent side triangles into an equality that yields right angles. Equality without bisection is insufficient: an isosceles trapezium can also have equal diagonals.','Locate a common midpoint and draw both equal diagonals through it. Join successive endpoints, not opposite endpoints. Use triangle congruence or the diagonal-square identity to justify the right angles. Vary the crossing angle to see many rectangles, including the square case.','Equal and bisecting: keep both conditions.','Side lengths=d sin(θ/2), d cos(θ/2)','Both diagonals have positive equal length and a crossing angle strictly between 0° and 180°.',[C('d','Common diagonal length',10,1,20),C('angle','Crossing angle in degrees',60,10,170)],{d:12,angle:90},'Half diagonal','Equal diagonals alone prove a rectangle.','An isosceles trapezium shows why their common midpoint must also be specified.','For common diagonal length {d}, what is the midpoint-to-endpoint length?');
P('quadrilateral-angles','A convex quadrilateral has interior-angle sum 360°. A diagonal divides it into two triangles, each with sum 180°.','The diagonal supplies the reason for the formula, not merely a way to memorize it. The fourth angle is determined once the other three are known. A proposed set of convex angles must keep each angle positive and below 180°.','Add the three known angles. Subtract the sum from 360°. Check the missing angle meets the intended shape conditions. If the question also specifies parallel sides, use supplementary and opposite-angle relationships as additional checks.','Two triangles: 180°+180°=360°.','A+B+C+D=360°','This tool models convex interior angles; invalid combinations are rejected.',[C('a','First angle',80,20,160),C('b','Second angle',100,20,160),C('c','Third angle',70,20,160)],{a:90,b:90,c:90},'Fourth angle','All quadrilaterals have four equal angles.','The sum is fixed, but individual angles may differ. Equal angles would each be 90°.','Find the fourth convex interior angle when the first three are {a}°, {b}° and {c}°.');
P('kite','A kite can be constructed with two pairs of adjacent equal sides. In the symmetric construction its diagonals are perpendicular, and one diagonal bisects the other.','Do not transfer every rhombus property to a kite. Unless the top and bottom halves agree, the horizontal diagonal does not bisect the vertical one. With perpendicular diagonals, four right triangles give area equal to half the product of the diagonal lengths.','Mark the symmetry axis and equal adjacent sides. Distinguish the full diagonals from their halves. Add the two vertical halves, double the horizontal half, then apply the perpendicular-diagonal area formula. Explain which bisection claim is supported.','A kite can have one bisecting diagonal without both bisecting.','Area=d₁d₂/2','The tool uses a symmetric convex kite with positive top, bottom and half-width values.',[C('top','Upper diagonal half',4,1,10),C('bottom','Lower diagonal half',7,1,10),C('half','Horizontal half-width',3,1,8)],{top:5,bottom:5,half:4},'Area','Every kite has two diagonals that bisect each other.','In this model the vertical halves can be unequal even though the horizontal halves are equal.','Find the kite area with vertical halves {top}, {bottom} and horizontal half-width {half}.');
P('trapezium','A trapezium has a pair of parallel sides. Its area is the mean of those parallel base lengths multiplied by their perpendicular separation.','Some definitions require exactly one parallel pair; others allow at least one. State the convention before deciding whether a parallelogram belongs to the class. The area formula follows by fitting two copies into a parallelogram. Sloping side length is not the perpendicular height.','Identify the two parallel bases and measure their perpendicular separation. Add the bases, halve, and multiply by height. Include square units. If comparing classifications, apply the stated convention consistently.','Average the bases; multiply by perpendicular height.','A=(a+b)h/2','The picture shows an isosceles trapezium for clarity, while the area formula applies to any trapezium with these base and height values.',[C('a','First parallel base',8,1,15),C('b','Second parallel base',4,1,15),C('h','Perpendicular height',3,1,8)],{a:10,b:6,h:5},'Area','The sloping side can be used as height.','Height is perpendicular to the bases. A sloping side usually has a different length.','Find trapezium area for parallel bases {a}, {b} and perpendicular height {h}.');
P('remainder','Division with remainder writes a nonnegative integer as n=dq+r, where d is positive and 0≤r<d.','The quotient counts complete groups and the remainder counts leftovers. The remainder restriction makes the representation unique: without it, shifting one group between q and r would create many descriptions. Multiples of d are exactly the cases with remainder zero.','Divide to count complete groups. Multiply the quotient by the divisor, then subtract from the dividend. Check the reconstruction and the remainder bounds. A general number with a specified remainder can be written dq+r with variable whole-number q.','Complete groups plus leftovers; leftover smaller than a group.','n=dq+r, 0≤r<d','Nonnegative integer dividend and positive integer divisor.',[C('n','Dividend',47,0,1000),C('d','Divisor / group size',5,1,20)],{n:83,d:7},'Remainder','The remainder may be as large as the divisor.','Once r≥d, another complete group can be removed, so the quotient was not complete.','Find the remainder when {n} is divided by {d}.');
P('parity','Even integers have form 2k, and odd integers have form 2k+1. These algebraic forms explain sum and product parity rules.','Even+even and odd+odd are even; even+odd is odd. A product with an even factor is even. Two odd factors multiply to an odd result because (2p+1)(2q+1)=2(2pq+p+q)+1. Checking examples helps find a rule but does not prove it for every integer.','Identify the parity of each input. Write its 2k or 2k+1 form and expand. Separate a factor of 2 from any leftover 1. Compare the tool output with this general proof, including zero as an even integer.','Even has no leftover after twos; odd has one.','even=2k; odd=2k+1','The controls use nonnegative integers; parity rules extend to all integers.',[C('a','First integer',7,0,50),C('b','Second integer',9,0,50)],{a:8,b:11},'Sum parity','A collection of examples proves a parity rule.','Use algebra to cover arbitrary integers; use a counterexample to disprove an overly broad claim.','Is the sum of {a} and {b} even or odd?');
P('divisibility','A divisibility test predicts a zero remainder using place value instead of long division. The correct test depends on the divisor.','For 3 and 9, every power of ten leaves remainder 1, so the digit sum has the same remainder as the full number. For 11, powers of ten alternate between remainders 1 and −1. For 4 and 8, the last two or three digits suffice because earlier place values are divisible by the divisor.','Choose the divisor first, apply its matching digit or suffix test, and verify with a remainder. Being divisible by 2 does not guarantee divisibility by 4. A rule is established from place-value algebra, while a single numerical check illustrates it.','Match the test to the divisor; place value explains why.','10≡1 mod 3,9; 10≡−1 mod 11','Positive whole-number digit strings; the tool checks divisors 2,3,4,5,8,9,10,11.',[C('n','Whole number to test',1232,1,99999)],{n:2466},'Digit sum','A digit-sum rule works for every divisor.','For 4 and 8 use suffixes; for 11 use alternating sums. The tests have different place-value reasons.','Find the digit sum of {n}, then use it to investigate divisibility by 3 and 9.');
P('cryptarithm','A cryptarithm replaces digits with letters while preserving arithmetic. Repeated letters mean the same digit; different letters normally mean different digits.','Leading digits cannot be zero. A carry into the tens column is a real constraint, not an extra independent choice. Solving a small puzzle means combining column arithmetic with digit uniqueness, then substituting the complete assignment back into the equation.','Choose or deduce the units digit, perform the addition or multiplication, and read the tens and units. Reject leading zero and repeated assignments to different letters. Record why a candidate fails rather than accepting only the numerical equality.','Same letter, same digit; different letter, different digit; no leading zero.','A+A=BC or A×A=BC','The tool handles these two small puzzle families; it is not a general cryptarithm solver.',[C('digit','Digit A',5,1,9),C('op','Operation: 1 addition, 2 multiplication',1,1,2)],{digit:4,op:2},'Result','Arithmetic equality alone makes a valid letter assignment.','The letter-to-digit rules must also hold. For example, a correct product may reuse A as C and violate distinctness.','With A={digit} and operation mode {op}, find the two-digit arithmetic result before checking assignment rules.');
P('distributive','The distributive law multiplies every term inside a bracket by the outside factor: a(b+c)=ab+ac.','An area split into two adjoining rectangles gives the same total as the unsplit rectangle when lengths are nonnegative. The algebraic identity also works for signed numbers. Using it for subtraction means distributing the minus sign or negative factor to every term.','Calculate the bracket total and multiply once. Separately calculate each partial product and add. Their equality checks the expansion. For a subtraction rewrite the second term with its sign before multiplying.','One outside factor visits every inside term.','a(b+c)=ab+ac','Numeric controls allow signed terms; physical area pictures apply only to nonnegative lengths.',[C('a','Outside factor',7,-10,20),C('b','First bracket term',8,-10,20),C('c','Second bracket term',2,-10,20)],{a:-3,b:5,c:-2},'Distributed value','Multiply only the first term inside a bracket.','Both terms must receive the outside factor. With a negative factor, both signs need attention.','Evaluate {a}({b}+{c}) by distributing first.');
P('identity','An algebraic identity is true for every permitted input. Squaring a sum produces a², two cross products ab, and b².','The four products in (a+b)(a+b) are a², ab, ba and b². Combining the two equal cross products gives 2ab. Replacing b with −b explains the square-of-difference identity. Multiplying a sum and difference cancels the cross terms instead.','Expand one factor against every term in the other. Collect like terms and keep their signs. Test a numerical example to catch a mistake, then use the symbolic expansion for the general explanation.','Square each, plus twice their product; change the middle sign for a difference.','(a±b)²=a²±2ab+b²; (a+b)(a−b)=a²−b²','Algebraic identities work for all real inputs. Area interpretations require nonnegative lengths.',[C('a','First value',7,-10,20),C('b','Second value',3,-10,20)],{a:9,b:1},'Cross term','The square of a sum is the sum of squares.','The two cross products are missing. Compare (7+3)²=100 with 7²+3²=58.','Find the cross term 2ab when a={a} and b={b}.');
P('ratio','A ratio compares quantities multiplicatively and preserves their order. Dividing both terms by the same positive factor produces an equivalent ratio.','A ratio 3:2 means three equal parts of the first quantity for two parts of the second. It does not mean their difference is always one. Units must be compatible before cancellation, and the denominator term must be nonzero when the ratio is viewed as a quotient.','Name the first and second quantities in order. Convert units if needed, remove a greatest common divisor, and build a ratio table by multiplying both terms by a common factor. Use a unit ratio to find a corresponding missing quantity.','Same scale on both terms; order still matters.','a:b=(a/g):(b/g); first=(a/b)×second','Positive whole-number ratio terms; the tool uses one already compatible unit.',[C('a','First ratio term',6,1,30),C('b','Second ratio term',4,1,30),C('n','New amount for the second quantity',20,1,100)],{a:9,b:6,n:30},'Corresponding first amount','Add the same number to both ratio terms to keep the ratio.','Adding usually changes the quotient. Multiply or divide both terms by the same nonzero factor.','A ratio is {a}:{b}. If the second quantity is {n}, find the first quantity.');
P('ratio-test','Two ratios form a proportion exactly when their cross products agree, provided the denominator terms are nonzero.','Cross multiplication follows by multiplying a/b=c/d by bd. It avoids the rounding error of comparing decimal approximations. Equal differences between ratio terms do not establish equal ratios.','Label the corresponding terms. Calculate ad and bc separately. Compare exact products, or solve a missing term from their equality. Substitute the solved value back into the original ratios to check correspondence.','Cross products equal means the ratios are equal.','a/b=c/d iff ad=bc','Denominators b and d must be nonzero; the controls use positive values.',[C('a','First numerator',2,1,20),C('b','First denominator',3,1,20),C('c','Second numerator',4,1,20),C('d','Second denominator',6,1,20)],{a:3,b:5,c:9,d:15},'First cross product','A matching difference means matching ratios.','2:3 and 4:5 both differ by 1 but have different quotients.','Find the cross product a×d for ratios {a}:{b} and {c}:{d}, then compare with b×c.');
P('share','Sharing a total in ratio a:b allocates a of its equal parts to one recipient and b to the other.','The total contains a+b parts, not a or b alone. One part equals total/(a+b). Multiplying that part size by each ratio term makes shares that preserve the ratio and add to the starting total.','Add the ratio terms. Divide the total by that sum. Multiply the part size by each term and check both the ratio and the sum. A share can be fractional unless the context requires indivisible objects.','Add the parts before dividing the total.','First=T a/(a+b); second=T b/(a+b)','Positive ratio terms and total. Physical indivisible items may require a compatible total.',[C('a','First ratio term',3,1,10),C('b','Second ratio term',2,1,10),C('total','Total to share',50,1,200)],{a:5,b:3,total:64},'First share','Divide the total by the first ratio term only.','The whole has a+b parts. Dividing by a ignores the second share.','Share {total} in ratio {a}:{b}. Find the first share.');
P('inverse','Inverse proportion keeps a product constant. If the same work is done by twice as many equally productive workers, the idealised time halves.','The model relies on the workload staying fixed. Setup costs, coordination overhead or unequal productivity can break the relationship. A falling quantity is not automatically inversely proportional: the exact constant-product relation must hold.','Calculate the original product of workers and time. Divide that workload by the new worker count. Check the new product equals the old product and explain whether the assumptions fit the real situation.','Fixed work: workers times time stays fixed.','w₁t₁=w₂t₂','Same fixed task, equally productive workers, no setup or coordination losses.',[C('a','Original workers',6,1,30),C('b','Original time in days',8,1,30),C('n','New workers',12,1,30)],{a:4,b:15,n:10},'New time','More workers always makes time directly proportional.','Direct proportion would increase time. For fixed work, inverse proportion decreases it under the stated assumptions.','{a} workers take {b} days. How many days would {n} equally productive workers take?');
P('affine','A relationship y=mx+k has a constant rate m and an initial value k. A nonzero initial value prevents direct proportion.','For a taxi fare with a fixed charge, doubling distance does not double the whole fare. Direct proportion is the special case k=0, where the graph goes through the origin and y/x stays constant. A straight line alone is not enough.','Separate the fixed charge from the per-unit charge. Multiply the unit rate by the quantity, then add the fixed term once. Compare output/input at several positive inputs to test whether it stays constant.','Straight line is not always direct proportion; check the origin.','y=mx+k; direct proportion requires k=0','This model assumes a constant rate and fixed charge; x is positive for ratio comparisons.',[C('a','Charge per unit',3,1,10),C('b','Fixed charge',5,0,20),C('n','Quantity',4,1,20)],{a:2,b:7,n:6},'Total','Any constant-increase relationship is proportional.','A nonzero fixed term changes the ratios even when the increments stay constant.','Find the total charge for {n} units at {a} per unit plus fixed charge {b}.');
P('density','Between two distinct rational numbers there is another rational number, and this construction can be repeated indefinitely. Irrational numbers also occur in every such interval.','The average of two rational endpoints is rational and lies strictly inside. For rational a<b, a+(b−a)/√2 is irrational and also inside because 0<1/√2<1. Displayed finite decimals approximate the latter; the formula establishes its irrationality.','Order the endpoints. Construct their average and verify both inequalities. Repeat on a subinterval to obtain more rationals. For an irrational example use an exact irrational expression, not a finite decimal claimed to be irrational.','Midpoint gives a rational; an exact irrational shift gives another kind.','m=(a+b)/2; u=a+(b−a)/√2','This demonstration uses rational endpoints represented by integers; a<b is required.',[C('a','Left rational endpoint',1,-10,10),C('b','Right rational endpoint',2,-10,10)],{a:3,b:5},'Rational midpoint','A nonterminating-looking calculator display proves irrationality.','A finite display is rational. Irrationality belongs to the exact number and needs an argument.','Find the rational midpoint between {a} and {b}.');
P('recurring','A purely recurring decimal repeats a fixed digit block forever. Multiplying by a power of ten aligns the repeated tails so they can be subtracted.','For a two-digit block, 100x and x have identical decimal tails. Subtracting leaves 99x equal to the block. The resulting fraction may reduce. A leading zero still counts as a digit in the repeating block.','Let x equal the recurring decimal. Count the block length k. Write 10ᵏx, subtract x, solve (10ᵏ−1)x=block and reduce by a common divisor. This tool covers purely recurring decimals; a nonrepeating prefix needs another subtraction setup.','Shift one full block; subtract the same tail.','x=block/(10ᵏ−1)','Purely recurring block only; 0≤block<10ᵏ, integer k.',[C('n','Repeating digit block',27,0,99),C('places','Digits in the block',2,2,2)],{n:45,places:2},'Reduced numerator','0.(09) has a one-digit block because the zero has no value.','Its block has two positions. The denominator is 99, giving 9/99=1/11.','For repeating block {n} with {places} digits, find the reduced fraction numerator.');
P('polynomial','A polynomial in x is a finite sum of terms with nonnegative integer powers of x. Coefficients are constants with respect to x.','A negative power such as x⁻¹ or a fractional power such as x^(1/2) is not a polynomial term. The degree is the largest exponent with nonzero coefficient after simplification. A zero leading coefficient can lower the degree, so inspect the actual expression rather than a label.','Simplify and combine like powers. Check each variable exponent, identify nonzero coefficients, and determine the degree. To evaluate, replace every occurrence of x with the same value and follow the order of operations.','Polynomial powers are whole and nonnegative; zero coefficients do not set degree.','p(x)=Σaᵢxⁱ, integer i≥0','Numeric experiment uses x>0 so negative/fractional powers are defined; the classification still follows the formal exponent rule.',[C('a','Leading coefficient',2,0,5),C('b','Linear coefficient',-3,-5,5),C('c','Constant term',4,-5,5),C('exponent','Leading exponent',2,-2,4,.5),C('x','Value of x',2,1,5)],{a:1,b:2,c:3,exponent:.5,x:4},'Value','The number of written terms gives the degree.','Degree concerns the largest nonzero exponent after combining terms, not the term count.','Evaluate {a}x^{exponent}+({b})x+({c}) at x={x}.');
P('coordinates','An ordered pair (x,y) locates a point by horizontal movement first and vertical movement second. The order carries meaning.','The horizontal axis is the x-axis; the vertical axis is the y-axis. Signs identify quadrants away from the axes. A point with x=0 lies on the y-axis, and a point with y=0 lies on the x-axis; neither belongs to a quadrant.','Start at the origin. Move horizontally by x, then vertically by y. Read the coordinates in that same order. Use a common scale and labelled axes when describing a map; interchanging coordinates usually changes the point.','Across first, up or down second.','P=(x,y)','Axes are perpendicular and use a stated uniform unit scale.',[C('x','Horizontal coordinate',-3,-10,10),C('y','Vertical coordinate',4,-10,10)],{x:5,y:-2},'Location','Every point belongs to one of the four quadrants.','Points on either axis are excluded from the quadrants. The origin lies on both axes.','Name the quadrant or axis location of ({x},{y}).');
P('linear-equation','A linear equation in two variables has form ax+by+c=0 with a and b not both zero. A solution is an ordered pair that makes the equation true.','One equation ordinarily leaves one degree of freedom, so it has infinitely many real solution pairs. If b≠0, each chosen x determines y. If b=0 and a≠0, x is fixed while y is free. A two-variable equation is different from a pair of equations solved simultaneously.','Substitute both coordinates and calculate the residual ax+by+c. Zero confirms a solution. To generate pairs, choose the free coordinate and solve for the other, checking any nonzero-divisor restriction before division.','A solution pair makes the residual zero.','ax+by+c=0; y=−(ax+c)/b when b≠0','a,b not both zero. The table solves for y only when b≠0.',[C('a','Coefficient of x',2,-5,5),C('b','Coefficient of y',1,-5,5),C('c','Constant',-7,-10,10),C('x','Test x',2,-5,5),C('y','Test y',3,-5,5)],{a:1,b:2,c:-8,x:2,y:3},'Residual','One equation in two variables always gives one pair.','It generally describes an entire line of pairs. A second independent condition is needed for a unique intersection.','Find ax+by+c for coefficients {a},{b},{c} and pair ({x},{y}).');
P('balance','An equality remains true when the same quantity is added to both sides. Common notions describe such fundamental relationships between equal quantities.','The reasoning is general: (a+c)−(b+c)=a−b. If a=b, the new difference is still zero. A numerical model can show preservation for chosen values; the algebra explains why it holds for all permitted values.','State the original equality and the operation applied equally. Write the changed expressions on both sides, simplify, and check the same difference remains. Do not apply different operations and expect equality to survive.','Same operation, both sides.','a=b implies a+c=b+c','Real-number arithmetic. A numerical example illustrates but does not replace a general proof.',[C('a','First quantity',5,-10,20),C('b','Second quantity',5,-10,20),C('c','Common added quantity',3,-10,20)],{a:8,b:8,c:-2},'New difference','A matching-looking drawing proves an axiom or theorem.','Specify the given statement and justify each step; a drawing and example only support intuition.','After adding {c} to both {a} and {b}, what is the difference between the new values?');
P('angle','An angle measures a turn between rays with a common endpoint. Acute, right, obtuse, straight and reflex angles describe different ranges of this turn.','Angles in a linear pair sum to 180°, while angles around a point sum to 360°. These totals come from the straight and full turns. A reflex angle is greater than 180° and less than 360°, not an unusually large acute angle.','Identify the shared vertex and reference rays. Read the degree measure, classify it by its range, and calculate a related angle only when its defining configuration applies. A sum rule alone does not identify a linear pair without the required geometry.','Straight turn 180°; full turn 360°.','Supplement=180°−θ; full-turn remainder=360°−θ','Controls allow 1–359°. A nonnegative supplement exists only through 180°.',[C('angle','Angle in degrees',60,1,359)],{angle:120},'Full-turn remainder','Angles adding to 180° must be adjacent.','They are supplementary numerically, but a linear pair additionally shares a vertex and side with opposite outer rays.','What full-turn remainder accompanies an angle of {angle}°?');
P('parallel-lines','Two nonvertical lines y=m₁x+k₁ and y=m₂x+k₂ share a direction when their slopes are equal. Distinct lines with that direction are parallel.','Equal slopes and equal intercepts describe the same line, not two distinct parallel lines. Unequal slopes give one intersection. This algebraic model supports the geometric theorem that lines parallel to the same line are parallel to each other when distinct.','Compare slopes before solving an intersection. If slopes match, compare intercepts. If they differ, solve (m₁−m₂)x=k₂−k₁, then substitute x into either line to find y. Remember that vertical lines require a separate x=constant form.','Same slope: compare intercepts before calling lines parallel.','x=(k₂−k₁)/(m₁−m₂) if slopes differ','Nonvertical slope-intercept lines only.',[C('a','First slope',2,-5,5),C('b','First intercept',1,-5,5),C('m2','Second slope',2,-5,5),C('k2','Second intercept',4,-5,5)],{a:1,b:0,m2:-1,k2:4},'Relationship','Equal slopes always give two parallel lines.','Equal intercepts as well make the two equations coincident. Distinctness matters.','Classify lines with slopes {a},{m2} and intercepts {b},{k2}.');
P('triangle','Congruent triangles have matching side lengths and angles under a correct vertex correspondence. Similar triangles preserve shape but may differ in size.','The order of corresponding vertices fixes which sides and angles are compared. SSS congruence uses three equal corresponding sides. Scaling every side by k preserves angles and multiplies lengths by k and areas by k²; only k=1 preserves size.','First check the three lengths form a triangle. Write corresponding vertices and side pairs in order. Use the stated criterion rather than the appearance of a diagram. CPCT applies after congruence is established, not as a reason to assume it.','Match vertices first; establish congruence before CPCT.','Similarity factor k: perimeter ×k, area ×k²','The tool scales one valid triangle, so it demonstrates SSS similarity/congruence; it does not prove every criterion or solve arbitrary triangle matching.',[C('a','First side',3,1,12),C('b','Second side',4,1,12),C('c','Third side',5,1,12),C('scale','Corresponding-side scale factor',1,.5,3,.5)],{a:5,b:5,c:6,scale:2},'Second perimeter','Equal angles alone prove congruence.','They can prove similarity, but size may differ. A scale factor of 2 is a counterexample to congruence.', 'Find the second triangle perimeter when sides {a},{b},{c} are scaled by {scale}.');
P('isosceles','In a triangle, equal sides oppose equal angles. Conversely, equal angles oppose equal sides. These are related but distinct theorem statements.','To use the converse, the given information must concern equal angles; you then infer equality of their opposite sides. With both base angles θ, the apex angle is 180°−2θ. The angle sum also limits a valid positive base angle to below 90°.','Name the equal angles and identify the sides opposite them. State the converse theorem, then conclude those sides are equal. Calculate the remaining angle from the triangle sum and verify it is positive.','Equal angles point to equal opposite sides.','Apex=180°−2θ','Equal positive base angles strictly below 90°.',[C('angle','Each base angle',50,10,80)],{angle:65},'Apex angle','The sides beside equal angles must be equal.','Compare opposite sides, not an arbitrary pair of adjacent sides.','Find the apex angle when both base angles are {angle}°.');
P('chord','The perpendicular from a circle centre to a chord bisects it. Radius, perpendicular distance and half-chord form a right triangle.','Within one circle or equal-radius circles, equal chords have equal distances from the centre and conversely. Radius must be held fixed for that comparison. A longer chord sits closer to the centre; the diameter corresponds to distance zero.','Draw the perpendicular, label the radius and half-chord, then use Pythagoras. Double the half-chord to get the full length. If comparing circles, check their radii before transferring an equal-distance conclusion.','Half a chord joins the centre-distance right triangle.','Half chord=√(r²−d²)','0≤d≤r and r>0. At d=r the chord degenerates to a contact point.',[C('radius','Circle radius',5,1,10),C('distance','Perpendicular centre distance',3,0,10)],{radius:13,distance:5},'Full chord','Equal centre distances give equal chords in any two circles.','The radii must also agree. The formula includes both r and d.','Find the full chord length for radius {radius} and centre distance {distance}.');
P('heron','Heron’s formula finds the area of a triangle from three side lengths without first finding an altitude. The semiperimeter is half the perimeter.','The strict triangle inequalities must hold, otherwise the lengths do not form a nondegenerate triangle. The radicand s(s−a)(s−b)(s−c) has units of length⁴, whose square root gives area in square units.','Check the triangle inequalities, calculate s, substitute each difference separately, and take the nonnegative square root. For a cost problem multiply the area by a per-square-unit rate, keeping length and area units distinct.','Check triangle, halve perimeter, multiply four factors, root.','A=√[s(s−a)(s−b)(s−c)], s=(a+b+c)/2','Positive side lengths satisfying strict triangle inequalities; same length unit.',[C('a','First side',13,1,20),C('b','Second side',14,1,20),C('c','Third side',15,1,20),C('rate','Cost per square unit',2,1,10)],{a:3,b:4,c:5,rate:5},'Area','Use the full perimeter as s.','Heron’s formula uses semiperimeter, not perimeter. For 3,4,5, s=6.','Find the triangle area from sides {a}, {b}, {c}.');
P('cone','A right circular cone has a base radius r, perpendicular height h and slant height l. Pythagoras links them: l²=r²+h².','Curved surface area uses slant height because it measures the lateral surface. Volume uses perpendicular height because it measures stacked cross-sections. Total area of a closed cone includes its circular base once. Confusing the two heights is a common source of correct-looking wrong answers.','Identify the height type in the problem. Compute l if needed, choose the requested area or volume formula, and keep units. Coefficients displayed as “/π” must be multiplied by π for the full measurement; they allow exact answers without choosing a decimal for π.','Area uses slant; volume uses straight height.','CSA=πrl; TSA=πr(l+r); V=πr²h/3','Right circular cone with positive radius/height; all lengths use one unit.',[C('radius','Base radius',3,1,10),C('h','Perpendicular height',4,1,12)],{radius:5,h:12},'Volume / π','Replace h by l in the volume formula.','Only the lateral area uses l. Cone volume is one third of a same-base, same-height cylinder.','Find the coefficient of π in cone volume for r={radius}, h={h}.');
P('sphere','A sphere has surface area 4πr² and volume 4πr³/3. A hemisphere has half the sphere’s volume but its total surface includes an additional flat disk.','Curved hemisphere area is 2πr². Closing its circular opening adds πr², giving total area 3πr². Radius and diameter differ by a factor of 2; substituting a diameter as a radius causes a factor-of-four area error and factor-of-eight volume error.','Read whether the object is a sphere or hemisphere and whether its surface is open or closed. Convert diameter to radius, select the formula, and use square units for area or cubic units for volume. Retain π for exact results.','Half the volume; curved half-area plus a disk if closed.','Sphere A=4πr², V=4πr³/3; hemisphere TSA=3πr²','Positive radius; ideal spherical geometry.',[C('radius','Radius',3,1,10)],{radius:6},'Hemisphere total area / π','Hemisphere total area is half the sphere area.','That gives only its curved area. Add the flat circular face when total area is requested.','Find the coefficient of π in the total area of a closed hemisphere of radius {radius}.');
P('cuboid','A cuboid has three pairs of equal opposite rectangular faces. Surface area adds the six faces; volume multiplies its three perpendicular dimensions.','Area and volume measure different quantities. A net unfolds the surface into rectangles, while a stack of unit cubes explains volume. Capacity can be expressed in litres: 1000 cm³ equals 1 litre, but 1000 cm² is not a capacity unit.','Put all dimensions in the same length unit. Compute ab, bh and ha for face areas, double their sum for total area, and multiply abh for volume. For a cube set all dimensions equal, reducing the formulas to 6s² and s³.','Three face areas twice; three lengths multiplied.','SA=2(ab+bh+ha); V=abh; 1000 cm³=1 L','Rectangular cuboid, positive dimensions, consistent units.',[C('a','Length',3,1,15),C('b','Width',4,1,15),C('h','Height',5,1,15)],{a:5,b:5,h:5},'Volume','Convert square centimetres to litres.','Litres measure volume. Use cubic centimetres and the volume conversion factor.','Find cuboid volume for dimensions {a}, {b}, {h}.');
P('cylinder','A right circular cylinder has a circular base repeated through a perpendicular height. Its curved surface unfolds into a rectangle with width 2πr and height h.','The unrolled rectangle explains curved area 2πrh. A closed cylinder has two circular ends, adding 2πr². Volume is base area πr² multiplied by height. Nets help identify which faces are actually exposed when an end is open or joined to another solid.','Determine whether the question asks curved area, total closed area or volume. Identify r and h, include only the requested surfaces, and state the unit. A coefficient of π is an exact representation rather than a decimal approximation.','Unroll for area; stack circular bases for volume.','CSA=2πrh; TSA=2πr(h+r); V=πr²h','Right circular cylinder, positive radius and height.',[C('radius','Radius',3,1,10),C('h','Height',4,1,15)],{radius:2,h:10},'Volume / π','Curved area includes the circular ends.','The two ends are separate faces. Add them only when the requested surface includes them.','Find the coefficient of π in cylinder volume for radius {radius} and height {h}.');
P('grouped','Grouped data record counts in intervals rather than the individual observations. Means, medians and modes calculated from such groups generally involve approximation.','Class midpoints stand in for unknown values when finding a grouped mean. The median class contains the halfway cumulative count; interpolation assumes observations are spread within that interval. A grouped mode formula additionally uses neighbouring class frequencies and normally equal class widths.','Build frequency and cumulative-frequency columns. For mean sum frequency times midpoint and divide by total. For median locate N/2 and interpolate using the previous cumulative count. For mode first identify a unique modal class and check the class-width assumptions.','Mean uses midpoints; median uses cumulative counts; mode uses the peak class.','Mean=Σfm/N; median=L+[(N/2−CF)/f]h','Three adjacent equal-width groups. Frequencies positive. The middle-class mode is shown only when it is uniquely modal.',[C('a','First-class frequency',2,1,20),C('b','Middle-class frequency',5,1,20),C('c','Last-class frequency',3,1,20),C('width','Common class width',10,1,20)],{a:3,b:8,c:5,width:5},'Estimated mean','Grouped means recover the exact mean of the unknown raw data.','Midpoints are substitutes. Different raw observations can produce the same frequency table but different exact means.','Find the midpoint-based mean for frequencies {a},{b},{c} in three groups of width {width} starting at zero.');
P('histogram','A histogram represents continuous grouped data with adjoining rectangles. When class widths differ, frequency must be represented by area rather than raw height.','Frequency density equals frequency divided by width. A wider interval with the same frequency needs a lower rectangle so its area remains equal to that frequency. The usual raw-frequency height is suitable only under a common-width convention.','Inspect class widths before choosing a vertical scale. Calculate density for every class, draw the interval width on the horizontal axis and density on the vertical axis, then verify width times height recovers the count. Label the scale clearly.','Histogram area counts; density supplies height.','density=frequency/width','Positive class widths; two-class comparison. This does not assume raw observations are uniformly distributed for inference.',[C('a','First frequency',12,1,40),C('b','Second frequency',18,1,40),C('w1','First width',10,1,30),C('w2','Second width',20,1,30)],{a:20,b:15,w1:5,w2:15},'First density','The highest histogram rectangle must have the largest frequency.','A narrow class can have greater density but fewer observations. Compare rectangle areas.','Find the first density from frequency {a} and width {w1}.');
P('hcf-lcm','The highest common factor is the greatest positive divisor shared by two positive integers. The least common multiple is their smallest positive common multiple.','Prime factorizations give HCF by taking minimum exponents and LCM by maximum exponents. For two positive integers their product equals HCF times LCM. Equal-group packing suggests HCF; coinciding repeating events suggest LCM.','Identify whether the problem concerns common grouping or a common repeat. Calculate HCF, then LCM=ab/HCF. Check both divisibility conditions and the product identity. The two-number identity should not be transferred unchanged to three numbers.','Minimum prime exponents for HCF; maximum for LCM.','HCF(a,b)LCM(a,b)=ab','Two positive integers only.',[C('a','First positive integer',12,1,100),C('b','Second positive integer',18,1,100)],{a:24,b:36},'LCM','HCF and LCM are interchangeable in word problems.','They solve different grouping/repetition questions. Read what must divide or be divided by the result.','Find LCM of {a} and {b}.');
P('quadratic','A quadratic equation ax²+bx+c=0 has a nonzero leading coefficient. Its discriminant D=b²−4ac determines the number of distinct real roots.','D>0 gives two distinct real roots; D=0 gives one repeated root; D<0 gives no real roots. A polynomial zero is an input giving output zero. The zero-sum and zero-product relations connect roots to coefficients and help construct a polynomial from known roots.','Identify a,b,c with signs, calculate D, and state the real-root case. Factor when possible or use the quadratic formula. Substitute each root into the original equation. In a context problem reject roots incompatible with the meaning, such as a negative physical length.','Discriminant decides; substitution checks; context selects.','x=(−b±√D)/(2a); sum=−b/a; product=c/a','a≠0. Tool displays real roots only; coefficients and evaluations can be checked even when D<0.',[C('a','Quadratic coefficient a',1,1,5),C('b','Linear coefficient b',-5,-10,10),C('c','Constant c',6,-10,10)],{a:1,b:4,c:4},'Discriminant','D=0 means zero is the root.','It means the two formula branches agree. For x²+4x+4, the repeated root is −2.','Find the discriminant of {a}x²+({b})x+({c})=0.');
P('linear-system','A pair of linear equations supplies two constraints on the same ordered pair. The lines can meet once, be parallel, or coincide.','A nonzero coefficient determinant gives a unique solution. With zero determinant, inspect the constants and zero rows before concluding there is no solution or infinitely many. Substitution and elimination are equivalent algebraic ways to preserve both requirements.','Write both equations with consistent variable order. Eliminate a variable or substitute an expression, solve, then substitute into both equations. A coefficient-ratio test needs care when denominators are zero; cross products and rank reasoning avoid dividing by zero.','One intersection, parallel contradiction, or the same line.','D=ad−bc; x=(pd−bq)/D; y=(aq−pc)/D','Two real linear equations; determinant division applies only when D≠0.',[C('a','First x coefficient',1,-5,5),C('b','First y coefficient',1,-5,5),C('p','First right-hand side',7,-10,10),C('c','Second x coefficient',2,-5,5),C('d','Second y coefficient',-1,-5,5),C('q','Second right-hand side',5,-10,10)],{a:2,b:1,p:8,c:1,d:-1,q:1},'Determinant','A zero determinant always means no solution.','Dependent equations can have infinitely many solutions. Compare constants and treat zero rows explicitly.','Calculate the coefficient determinant ad−bc for coefficients {a},{b},{c},{d}.');
P('ap','An arithmetic progression adds the same common difference at every step. The nth term lies n−1 steps after the first term.','The sum of the first n terms can be found by pairing the first with the last, the second with the next-to-last and so on. Every such pair has the same total. An increasing sequence is not necessarily arithmetic: its consecutive differences must agree.','Identify the first term a and difference d. Use a+(n−1)d for a position query. For a sum compute the last term, then n times the average of first and last. If solving for n, require a positive integer and check the term really occurs.','Nth term has n−1 gaps; sum is count times end-average.','aₙ=a+(n−1)d; Sₙ=n(a+aₙ)/2','Positive integer n and a constant real difference d.',[C('a','First term',3,-10,20),C('d','Common difference',4,-5,10),C('n','Number / position of terms',5,1,20)],{a:10,d:-2,n:6},'Nth term','Use a+nd for the nth term.','That takes n steps instead of n−1. For n=1, the formula must return a.','Find term {n} of an AP starting at {a} with difference {d}.');
P('similarity','Similarity preserves corresponding angles and one common side-length ratio. It permits a change of size while retaining shape.','If every length scales by k, perimeter scales by k and area by k². AA similarity uses two corresponding angle equalities; SSS uses three equal side ratios; SAS needs the included angle between the proportional sides. The word corresponding fixes which ratios may be compared.','Match vertices, select the applicable criterion, and write ratios in a consistent direction. Solve for a missing length using the common factor. Square the factor for area comparisons and do not confuse similarity with congruence.','Angles keep shape; one factor scales all lengths; its square scales area.','Length factor k; area factor k²','Positive uniform scale. The tool constructs already-similar shapes and illustrates consequences; it does not by itself prove a criterion.',[C('a','First reference length',3,1,10),C('b','Second reference length',4,1,10),C('scale','Uniform scale factor',2,.5,3,.5)],{a:5,b:6,scale:3},'Area factor','Equal ratios from mismatched sides establish similarity.','The vertex correspondence must be consistent. SSS also needs all three ratios, not a selected pair.','Find the area factor when all corresponding lengths scale by {scale}.');
P('bpt','A line parallel to one side of a triangle divides the other two sides in the same ratio. The converse can establish parallelism from equality of those ratios.','One proof compares areas of triangles sharing heights or bases, then uses equal-altitude triangles on the parallel lines. Another formulation uses similar triangles. The forward theorem requires the parallel premise; the converse begins with proportional divisions. A drawn parallel-looking segment is not the premise.','Label the four side segments in the same direction. Write AD/DB=AE/EC and solve by cross multiplication. For a converse problem check both ratios, then state why the line is parallel. Do not replace a side segment with the whole side accidentally.','Parallel cut gives equal splits; equal splits can prove parallel cut.','AD/DB=AE/EC; AD/AB=AE/AC','Positive internal side segments in a nondegenerate triangle; the tool constructs a parallel cut.',[C('a','AD',3,1,10),C('b','DB',2,1,10),C('c','AE',6,1,15)],{a:4,b:3,c:8},'EC','Use AD/DB=AE/AC.','The denominators must correspond. Split-to-split ratios and part-to-whole ratios are different but each can be consistent.','For a parallel cut, AD={a}, DB={b}, AE={c}. Find EC.');
P('distance','The distance between two Cartesian points is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs are their coordinate differences.','Subtraction may give a negative difference, but its square is nonnegative. The distance is the nonnegative square root. A midpoint instead averages coordinates, which is a different operation. Distance comparisons can identify equal sides and test a proposed right angle through squared lengths.','Subtract x-coordinates and y-coordinates in a consistent order. Square and add, then root. For a ratio or collinearity problem identify whether distance, midpoint or a section formula is needed before calculating. Repeated points require special care in a shape classification.','Subtract, square, add, root. Midpoint averages instead.','d=√[(x₂−x₁)²+(y₂−y₁)²]','Cartesian perpendicular axes with a common unit scale.',[C('x','First x',1,-8,8),C('y1','First y',2,-8,8),C('x2','Second x',4,-8,8),C('y2','Second y',6,-8,8)],{x:-2,y1:1,x2:4,y2:9},'Distance','Add coordinates to find distance.','Distance uses changes between points, not their position sum. Adding and halving gives a midpoint.','Find the distance from ({x},{y1}) to ({x2},{y2}).');
P('trig','In a right triangle, sine is opposite/hypotenuse, cosine is adjacent/hypotenuse, and tangent is opposite/adjacent relative to a chosen acute angle.','Similar right triangles explain why the ratios depend on the angle rather than the overall size. Pythagoras divided by hypotenuse² gives sin²θ+cos²θ=1. Reciprocal ratios require nonzero denominators. At endpoint angles some ratios are undefined, so the tool reports that rather than a huge misleading number.','Choose the reference angle before naming opposite and adjacent sides. Select the ratio that uses the known and requested sides. Keep degree units explicit. For exact special angles use the 30–60–90 or 45–45–90 triangle values, distinguishing those from rounded decimals.','SOH-CAH-TOA; reference angle first.','sin²θ+cos²θ=1; tanθ=sinθ/cosθ','Degrees 0–90. Endpoint zero denominators are undefined; numerical ratios are approximations.',[C('angle','Angle in degrees',30,0,90)],{angle:60},'Sine','The opposite side stays the same when the reference angle changes.','Switching between the two acute angles swaps opposite and adjacent. The hypotenuse stays opposite the right angle.','Find sin({angle}°), giving a decimal if needed.');
P('height','A height-and-distance problem converts an angle of elevation into the vertical rise above the observer’s horizontal eye line.','Tangent relates that rise to horizontal distance. Eye height is added afterwards if the question asks total height above the same level ground. Angle of depression is measured downward from a horizontal reference, not from a vertical line. Unequal ground levels need an additional correction.','Draw a labelled right triangle. Identify horizontal distance, angle and observer height. Compute distance×tanθ for the rise and add the eye height once. Check units and decide whether the physical assumptions are justified.','Tangent gives rise above eye level; then add eye height.','H=d tanθ+eye height','Level ground, vertical object and measured horizontal distance; 0<θ<90°.',[C('distance','Horizontal distance',20,1,50),C('angle','Elevation in degrees',30,5,80),C('eye','Observer eye height',1.5,0,2,.1)],{distance:10,angle:45,eye:1.6},'Total height','Tangent directly gives the whole object height in every setup.','It gives the rise above the horizontal sightline. The observer’s eye level can make the total different.','Find total object height for distance {distance}, elevation {angle}° and eye height {eye}.');
P('tangent','A tangent touches a circle at one point and is perpendicular to the radius at that contact point. An external point has two tangent segments of equal length.','The radius and tangent segment form a right triangle with the centre-to-external-point distance as hypotenuse. An inside point has no real tangent; a point on the circle has one tangent line. Counting tangent lines is different from treating the zero-length on-circle segment as an external construction.','Compare OP with radius r first. For an external point compute √(OP²−r²). Use the equal-tangent theorem for other lengths, and use the radius perpendicularity only at the actual contact point. Verify the radicand is nonnegative.','Radius is perpendicular at contact; external tangents have equal lengths.','PT²=OP²−r²','Ideal circle with r>0; tangent segment formula requires OP≥r.',[C('radius','Circle radius',3,1,10),C('distance','Distance from centre to point',5,1,15)],{radius:5,distance:13},'Number of tangents','Every point determines two tangents.','Inside gives none, on-circle gives one line, and outside gives two. Location must be checked first.','How many tangent lines pass through a point with OP={distance} and circle radius {radius}?');
P('sector','A sector uses a fraction θ/360 of a full circle’s area and circumference. A segment is the region between a chord and its arc.','For a minor central angle through 180°, subtract the isosceles centre triangle from the sector to get the minor segment. Area and arc length are different quantities with different units. A major segment can be found by subtracting the corresponding minor segment from the full circle.','Identify whether the region is a sector, segment or combination. Compute the full-circle quantity and multiply by the angle fraction. For a segment remove the centre triangle using its area, then check whether a major/minor complement is requested.','Sector is a slice; segment is slice minus triangle.','Sector=(θ/360)πr²; arc=(θ/360)2πr','θ measured in degrees. Minor-segment subtraction shown only for 0≤θ≤180°.',[C('radius','Radius',6,1,12),C('angle','Central angle in degrees',60,1,180)],{radius:4,angle:90},'Sector area / π','Arc length and sector area use the same circle factor.','Arc scales circumference 2πr, while area scales πr². Their units differ.','Find the coefficient of π in a sector area for radius {radius} and angle {angle}°.');
P('composite-solid','A composite solid joins simpler solids. Its volume can add nonoverlapping component volumes, but its exposed surface excludes internal joined faces.','For a cone joined to a hemisphere along the same circular base, the disk is internal to the combined object. Adding both closed total areas would count that internal disk twice. Volume addition is valid only when the component interiors do not overlap.','Sketch the components and mark exposed versus joined faces. Add the required curved or flat exposed surfaces only. Calculate component volumes separately and add. Use a common radius and length unit, and check whether the question concerns surface material or capacity.','Volumes add; hidden joined faces do not count as exposed area.','Exposed area=πrl+2πr²; V=πr²h/3+2πr³/3','Tool models one cone plus one hemisphere with a shared radius and nonoverlapping interiors.',[C('radius','Shared radius',3,1,10),C('h','Cone height',4,1,12)],{radius:5,h:12},'Exposed area / π','Add the two closed total surface areas unchanged.','Their common base is hidden. Remove both counted copies of that disk from the surface sum.','Find the coefficient of π in the exposed area of the joined cone/hemisphere with radius {radius} and cone height {h}.');
P('probability','The classical counting formula P(E)=favourable/total requires a finite sample space of equally likely outcomes.','The probability of an impossible event is 0 and of a certain event is 1. Every probability lies between them. An event and its complement partition the entire sample space, giving probabilities that add to 1. “Favourable” means meeting the specified event, not a value judgment.','List or count outcomes systematically, check equal likelihood, count the event outcomes, and form the fraction. For a complement count the remaining outcomes or subtract from 1. If outcomes have unequal weights, add their probabilities instead of counting them equally.','Count only after checking equally likely.','P(E)=m/N; P(not E)=1−P(E)','Finite equally likely sample space; 0≤m≤N.',[C('a','Favourable outcomes',3,0,6),C('n','Total equally likely outcomes',6,1,20)],{a:4,n:10},'Event probability','Every listed outcome has equal probability automatically.','Equal likelihood is an assumption to justify. A biased coin or unequal region sizes can invalidate simple counts.','Find event probability with {a} favourable outcomes among {n} equally likely outcomes.');
P('coins','Repeated fair independent coin tosses produce ordered outcomes. For k tosses there are 2ᵏ equally likely strings of heads and tails.','HT and TH are different ordered outcomes even though both contain one head. Treating the counts 0,1,...,k as equally likely is wrong. A tree or systematic binary list prevents missing or double-counting strings. Independence and fairness justify equal weights.','List both branches at each toss and keep the order. Count strings meeting the event condition, divide by 2ᵏ, and check the complement. Compare this theoretical result with an experiment without expecting a small sample to match exactly.','Order counts: HT is different from TH.','Total ordered outcomes=2ᵏ','Fair independent tosses; tool supports 1–6 tosses and a feasible target head count.',[C('k','Number of tosses',2,1,6),C('heads','Required heads',1,0,6)],{k:3,heads:2},'Probability','The possible head counts are equally likely.','For two coins, 0 and 2 heads each have one string, while 1 head has two.','Find probability of exactly {heads} heads in {k} fair independent tosses.');
P('monomial','A monomial is a single product of a coefficient and nonnegative integer variable powers. Multiplying monomials multiplies coefficients and adds exponents for each common variable.','The common-base rule explains the exponent addition. Different variables keep separate powers. Multiplying a monomial by a polynomial requires distributing it to every term; the numerical experiment checks one shared-variable product.','Multiply the signed coefficients first. Group equal variables, add their exponent counts, and retain distinct variables separately. Substitute a numerical value afterwards to verify that the original product and simplified expression agree.','Coefficients multiply; common-variable exponents add.','(axᵖ)(bxᑫ)=abxᵖ⁺ᑫ','Nonnegative integer powers for polynomial monomials.',[C('a','First coefficient',2,-5,5),C('b','Second coefficient',3,-5,5),C('p','First exponent',2,0,4),C('q','Second exponent',3,0,4),C('x','Numerical x',2,1,4)],{a:-3,b:2,p:1,q:2,x:3},'Coefficient','Add coefficients when multiplying monomials.','Addition combines like terms, while multiplication multiplies their coefficients. Keep the operation distinct.','Find the coefficient when ({a}x^{p}) and ({b}x^{q}) are multiplied.');
P('factor-quadratic','Factorisation reverses expansion: a polynomial is expressed as a product whose multiplication recreates it.','For x²+sx+p, candidate constants a,b need both a+b=s and ab=p. One condition alone is insufficient. Regrouping also uses common factors: ax+ay+bx+by=a(x+y)+b(x+y)=(a+b)(x+y). A zero of a factored polynomial makes at least one factor zero.','Look for a common factor first, then use an identity or two-number sum/product search. Expand your proposed factors to check every coefficient. When the original expression is divided, retain exclusions from any cancelled denominator factors.','Factors must expand back to every original term.','(x+a)(x+b)=x²+(a+b)x+ab','This tool illustrates monic quadratic binomial factors; more general leading coefficients require another grouping step.',[C('a','First factor constant',2,-8,8),C('b','Second factor constant',3,-8,8),C('x','Check input x',1,-5,5)],{a:-1,b:4,x:2},'Constant','Matching the constant term is enough to validate factors.','The middle coefficient must also match their sum. Always expand back.','Find the constant term when (x+{a})(x+{b}) is expanded.');
P('monomial-division','Dividing monomials divides coefficients and subtracts exponents for the same variable. Cancellation does not remove the original domain restrictions.','If a denominator contains x, the original expression is undefined at x=0 even when the simplified form appears defined there. A negative exponent in the quotient signals a remaining variable denominator, so the result is not necessarily a polynomial.','Factor numerator and denominator, cancel matching nonzero factors, and subtract variable exponents. State excluded inputs from the original denominator. Multiply the quotient by the divisor to check the algebra where it is defined.','Cancel factors, keep exclusions.','axᵖ/(bxᑫ)=(a/b)xᵖ⁻ᑫ','b≠0 and x≠0 for the chosen divisor; integer powers.',[C('a','Numerator coefficient',6,1,12),C('b','Denominator coefficient',2,1,6),C('p','Numerator exponent',4,0,6),C('q','Denominator exponent',2,0,6),C('x','Nonzero x',2,1,5)],{a:9,b:3,p:2,q:4,x:3},'Exponent','Cancellation makes every formerly excluded input valid.','Simplification preserves equality only on the original domain. Keep x=0 excluded when required.','Find the remaining x exponent in ({a}x^{p})/({b}x^{q}).');
P('line-graph','A line graph connects values in an ordered quantitative sequence. For a constant-rate model y=mx+k, slope m measures output change per input unit.','Position on a graph and rate are different. A high position does not mean a high slope. In distance-time contexts the slope has units of distance/time, and a fixed initial value shifts the graph without changing that constant rate. Discrete or discontinuous data should not be connected indiscriminately.','Label both axes and units, choose an even scale, plot ordered pairs, and interpret the change between points. For this affine model read the intercept at zero and compute rise/run for its rate. Explain whether interpolation between observations is justified.','Height gives value; slope gives rate.','y=mx+k; slope=Δy/Δx','Tool displays a continuous affine model on inputs 0–5. Real data need their own interpolation assumptions.',[C('a','Rate / slope',3,-5,8),C('b','Initial output',2,-5,10),C('x','Input value',4,0,10)],{a:2,b:5,x:3},'Output','A graph higher up must represent a faster change.','Compare slopes, not vertical position. The initial value can raise a slow-changing line.','Find output at x={x} when y={a}x+({b}).');
